<main>
  <div class="page-header">
    <h1>Global SST and El Ni&ntilde;o/La Ni&ntilde;a Monitor</h1>
    <p>Daily sea-surface temperature anomalies from NOAA's Optimum Interpolation SST (OISST v2.1), the standard 1/4&deg; daily blended satellite-and-in-situ analysis. Anomalies are relative to the 1991&ndash;2020 climatology. Updated once daily as new OISST data lands (roughly a one-day latency).</p>
    <p>The maps show the current global anomaly field and a tropical Pacific zoom where ENSO signals are clearest. Below, the Relative Oceanic Ni&ntilde;o Index (RONI) tracks ENSO after removing the background tropical-mean warming.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-stack">

    <!-- SST ANOMALY MAPS -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Global &amp; Tropical Pacific SST Anomalies</h2>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=anomaly"
              title="Global and tropical Pacific SST anomaly animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA OISST v2.1 (PSL) &middot; base 1991&ndash;2020 &middot; 60-day animation, latest day shown</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="sst-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ABSOLUTE SST -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Absolute Sea-Surface Temperature</h2>
        <p>The actual SST field (not anomaly), global and tropical Pacific. The warm pool, equatorial cold tongue, and western boundary currents are visible directly; watch them shift day to day.</p>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=absolute"
              title="Absolute SST animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA OISST v2.1 (PSL) &middot; absolute SST &middot; 60-day animation</span>
        <span class="chart-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- RELATIVE SST (global-mean removed) -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>SST Anomaly &mdash; Global-Mean Removed</h2>
        <p>The anomaly with the area-weighted global-mean SST anomaly subtracted at every point. This strips out the uniform background-warming signal and isolates the <i>pattern</i> of warming and cooling &mdash; the El Ni&ntilde;o footprint stands out against a more balanced global ocean.</p>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=relative"
              title="Global-mean-removed SST anomaly animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA OISST v2.1 (PSL) &middot; anomaly minus global-mean anomaly &middot; 60-day animation</span>
        <span class="chart-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- SUBSURFACE CROSS-SECTION -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Equatorial Pacific Subsurface Temperature</h2>
        <p>Depth&ndash;longitude cross-section along the equator (0&deg;N) from the TAO/TRITON moored-buoy array: temperature (top) and its anomaly versus the 1991&ndash;2020 average (bottom), 5-day smoothed.</p>
        <p><b>What to look for:</b> The thermocline (here the 26&deg;C and 28&deg;C isotherms) tilts upward toward the east. Subsurface warm or cool anomalies along the thermocline often <i>lead</i> the surface ENSO signal &mdash; watch them build and propagate eastward in the animation.</p>
      </div>
      <iframe class="anim-embed subsurface" src="sst_anim.html?embed=1&amp;region=equatorial"
              title="Equatorial Pacific subsurface temperature animation" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">NOAA/PMEL TAO/TRITON (DISDEL) &middot; base 1991&ndash;2020 &middot; 120-day animation, latest shown</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="subsurface-updated">__TAO_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- EQUATORIAL 10m WIND HOVMÖLLER -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Equatorial Pacific 10 m Wind Forecast</h2>
        <p>Forecast 10 m zonal-wind anomaly (5&deg;S&ndash;5&deg;N), longitude &times; forecast day, from the AIFS-ENS (AI) and ECMWF IFS-ENS (physics) ensemble means vs. the ERA5 1991&ndash;2020 climatology.</p>
        <p><b>What to look for:</b> Westerly (red) anomalies along the equator push warm water eastward and favor El Ni&ntilde;o development; easterly (blue) anomalies favor La Ni&ntilde;a. Agreement between the AI and physics ensembles raises confidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/eq_wind_hovmoller.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Equatorial Pacific 10 m zonal-wind anomaly Hovmöller forecast (AIFS-ENS and IFS-ENS)" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">ECMWF AIFS-ENS &amp; IFS-ENS (open data) &middot; anomaly vs ERA5 1991&ndash;2020 &middot; updated each 00/12Z cycle</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- RONI -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Relative Oceanic Ni&ntilde;o Index (RONI)</h2>
        <p>RONI is an ENSO index that subtracts the tropical-mean SST anomaly (20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N) from the Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 anomaly, then applies a 3-month running mean. By removing the slowly-rising tropical background, it isolates the ENSO signal more cleanly than the conventional ONI &mdash; increasingly useful as overall ocean temperatures trend upward.</p>
        <p><b>How to read it:</b> Red bars (positive) lean El Ni&ntilde;o, blue bars (negative) lean La Ni&ntilde;a; the dashed lines mark the &plusmn;0.5&deg;C guides. This index is computed directly from OISST and shown in &deg;C &mdash; it captures the shape and timing of CPC's RONI but is not their standardized published value.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap">
        <img src="assets/sst/roni.webp?v=__CACHE__"
             alt="Relative Oceanic Niño Index time series" loading="lazy">
      </div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">Computed from NOAA OISST v2.1 &middot; Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 minus tropical-mean &middot; 3-mo running mean</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="roni-updated">__RONI_MONTH__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- DAILY 3-METRIC -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Daily Equatorial Pacific SST Indices</h2>
        <p>All three daily metrics on one axis: the Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 anomaly (the basis of ONI), the tropical-mean anomaly (20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N), and their difference &mdash; the daily relative index that underlies RONI. These are single-day values, so they are noisier than the 3-month running indices above, but they show the most recent evolution.</p>
        <p><b>What to look for:</b> When the red (Ni&ntilde;o-3.4) and green (tropical-mean) lines move together, the blue relative line stays flat &mdash; that's background warming, not ENSO. The blue line separating from zero is the genuine ENSO signal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap">
        <img src="assets/sst/daily_indices.webp?v=__CACHE__"
             alt="Daily Niño-3.4, tropical-mean, and relative SST indices" loading="lazy">
      </div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer">
        <span class="chart-sources">Daily, computed from NOAA OISST v2.1 &middot; year-to-date</span>
        <span class="chart-updated" id="daily-updated">__SST_DAY__</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- EVENT COMPARISON: Niño-3.4 evolution overlay -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>This Event vs. 1997, 2015 &amp; 2023</h2>
        <p>Niño-3.4 monthly anomaly through each event's development year and the following year &mdash; the current event (bold red) overlaid on the three most recent strong/super El Niños.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/events_nino34.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Niño-3.4 evolution: current event vs 1997/2015/2023" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">OISST v2.1 monthly &middot; anomaly vs 1991&ndash;2020</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- EVENT COMPARISON: multi-region flavor bars -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>El Niño Flavor &mdash; Niño Regions vs. Analogs</h2>
        <p>Niño-1+2 / 3 / 3.4 / 4 anomalies at the same calendar month as the latest data, for the current event and the three analogs. Strong eastern (1+2, 3) relative to central (4) marks an East-Pacific-flavored event.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/events_bars.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Niño-region anomalies, current event vs analogs" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">OISST v2.1 monthly &middot; same-phase comparison</span></div>
    </div>

    <!-- EVENT COMPARISON: matching-phase maps -->
    <div class="chart-card">
      <div class="chart-card-header">
        <h2>Matching-Phase SST Anomaly Maps</h2>
        <p>Global SST anomaly for the latest available month vs. the same calendar month in 1997, 2015 and 2023. Note how much warmer the global baseline is now than in 1997 &mdash; the same ENSO state now sits on a hotter ocean.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="img-wrap"><img src="assets/sst/events_maps.webp?v=__CACHE__" alt="Matching-phase global SST anomaly maps" loading="lazy"></div>
      <div class="chart-card-footer"><span class="chart-sources">OISST v2.1 monthly &middot; anomaly vs 1991&ndash;2020</span></div>
    </div>

  </div>

  <div class="colophon">
    <h3>About these plots</h3>
    <p>All three images are regenerated once daily by a scheduled job that pulls the latest OISST anomaly fields from NOAA PSL, renders the maps, computes the relative Ni&ntilde;o index from the same data, and commits the results. The RONI is calculated rather than fetched: it is the cosine-latitude-weighted Ni&ntilde;o-3.4 box-mean anomaly minus the 20&deg;S&ndash;20&deg;N tropical-mean anomaly, smoothed with a 3-month running mean.</p>
    <p>Source on <a href="https://github.com/scorvec/scorvec.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>.</p>
  </div>
</main>

<footer>
  <p class="counter">Visitors: <span id="count">&mdash;</span></p>
</footer>

<script>
  fetch('https://api.counterapi.dev/v1/scorvec/visits/up')
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(d => { document.getElementById('count').textContent = d.count.toLocaleString(); })
    .catch(() => { document.getElementById('count').textContent = '\u2014'; });
</script>
</body>
